 "notes": "Libretto by Lorenzo Da Ponte (1790). Words follow the Peters engraving's spelling and punctuation: degl' infami (printed degl 'infami), quest' alma and nell' amor as two words, and speraza in bars 119–126, where the print sets the long run on speranza as spe-ra-…-za and leaves out the n; the word text keeps the print's letters so it lines up with the notes, and its IPA and meaning are those of speranza. The first sung word, -ciamo? (bar 1, Dorabella), ends her Sorella, che facciamo? from the recitative before (Che susurro, che strepito), which shows and speaks that line whole, so it is left out of the phrases here; Dorabella sings nothing else in this piece. IPA, word meanings and translations come from the app's reviewed Così libretto drafts (L28 and L29, IPA checked against Castel). IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, elisions (two words on one note in the score) joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ. Translation literal, for meaning.",
